{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSBzgs4dTcfVAUMKwtgH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've fixed the integration. Three separate bugs were breaking it, and any one of them alone was enough. I tested the full flow against a local fake Intervals server, not against the real intervals.icu, so it still needs one run with the real client and an athlete account.\n\n1. **The Connect button got \"unauthorized\" for everyone.** The button was a plain link, and clicking a link loads a new page without sending the sign-in token, so the server rejected every athlete. Now the button asks the server for the Intervals login address through the app's normal signed-in request, then sends the browser there.\n2. **Intervals rejected the login page itself.** When calendar sync shipped on 2026-10-02, the app started asking for both read and write access to activities and to the calendar. Intervals refuses a request that names the same area twice, because write already includes read. That's why pasting the link by hand stopped working too. The app now asks for `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\n3. **Working connections were marked \"expired\" after an hour.** Intervals tokens don't expire, but the app assumed a one-hour lifetime. A background job then marked the connection expired because there was no renewal token to use. That is what happened to Rivera. I removed the made-up lifetime and the background job. Now, if Intervals rejects the token because the athlete revoked access, the app clears the token and shows the Connect button again.\n\nIn the test, the full connect flow worked, wellness data came back, and after a simulated revoke the app showed \"revoked, reconnect\" instead of failing.\n\nOf the colleague's note, the parts about the button, the duplicate scopes and the non-expiring tokens matched our causes. Our sign-in check on the return from Intervals already did what the note describes, so I left it alone. I didn't follow the note's advice to strip extra fields from the token exchange, because Rivera's successful connection used them. Encrypting stored tokens and checking that a reconnect uses the same Intervals account would be sensible next steps, but neither caused these failures. `ANSWER.md` has the details.\n\nThe changes are in `server.py`, `config.py` and `static/app.js`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1970,"cache_read_input_tokens":36355,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1970},"output_tokens":628,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"946029b2-0626-444a-9100-9648f510e32e","uuid":"348f0cc7-8914-4f67-a5b6-e845ab390dad","timestamp":"2026-10-27T05:29:03.430Z","request_id":"req_011CgSBzgUkGgom45sisSPkw"}
